Reproduced under `skills/agentic-patterns-research/references/` and
`skills/agentic-patterns-extra/references/` -- approximately 400 files,
measured by line-level comparison against the fetched corpus. Most are
verbatim or near-verbatim copies rather than paraphrase.

Per-directory provenance, including the upstream revision each batch was taken
from, is recorded in the accompanying `raw/*-docs/INDEX.md` files:

| Destination | Files that are substantially copies | Origin disclosed before 2026-10-11? |
|---|---|---|
| `skills/agentic-patterns-research/references/` | 233 of 234 | yes -- 221 carry a `Source:` line |
| `skills/agentic-patterns-extra/references/` | ~168 of 195 | **ambiguously** -- see below |

The second row needs care because a `source:` key there does **not** mean
"copied from". In that corpus `source:` names the *subject* a document
discusses -- a YouTube explainer, a blog post, a product page -- so a file that
reproduces upstream documentation verbatim looked identical in shape to one
written here. A redistributer scanning for `source:` would have found a YouTube
link and concluded the text was original.

That is why 168 of those files now carry a `Derived from:` line instead: the
wording is deliberately different from `source:`, because reusing the same word
would have rebuilt the ambiguity. It sits **below** the YAML frontmatter block
for the same reason -- at line 1 a `---` block would stop parsing.

Files with no provable upstream origin were left alone: `INDEX.md`,
`code-then-execute.md`, `budget-aware-model-routing.md` and
`adaptive-sandbox-fan-out-controller.md`. Roughly 20 more are partial
rewritings (8-58% line overlap with their source) rather than copies, and were
also left unmarked rather than labelled on suspicion alone.

